WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.140
This is a standard color photo made with a
smartphone.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:13.669
Hence, it contains only a 2D representation
of the world, and when we look at it, our

00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:17.890
brain is able to reconstruct the 3D information
from it.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:24.179
And I wonder, would it be possible for an
AI to do the same, and go all the way and

00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:28.039
create a 3D version of this photo that we
can rotate around?

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:34.129
Well, this new learning-based method promises
exactly that, and if that is at all possible,

00:00:34.129 --> 00:00:35.379
even more.

00:00:35.379 --> 00:00:39.760
These are big words, so let’s have a look
if it can indeed live up to its promise.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.309
So, first, we take a photograph, and we’ll
find out together in a moment what kind of

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:47.230
phone is needed for this.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.660
Probably an amazing one, right?

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:55.379
For now, this will be the input, and now,
let’s see the 3D photo as an output.

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:57.480
Let’s rotate this around.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.750
And…wow.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.129
This is amazing.

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:02.239
And you know what is even more amazing?

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:07.450
Since pretty much every smartphone is equipped
with a gyroscope, these photos can be rotated

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:13.790
around in harmony with the rotation of our
phones, and wait a second…is this some sort

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:19.640
of misunderstanding, or do I see correctly
that we can even look behind a human if we

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:20.960
wanted to.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.799
That content was not even part of the original
photo!

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:26.240
How does this work?

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:27.810
More on that in a moment.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:34.240
Also, just imagine putting on a pair of VR
glasses, and looking at a plain 2D photo and

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.200
get an experience as if we were really there.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.280
It truly feels like we are living in a science
fiction world.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.560
If we grab our trusty smartphone and use these
images, we can create a timeline full of these

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:52.780
3D photos and marvel at how beautifully we
can scroll such a timeline here.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.130
And now, we have piled up quite a few questions
here.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.380
How is this wizardry possible?

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.560
What kind of phone do we need for this?

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.340
Do we need a depth sensor?

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.390
Maybe even LiDAR?

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:08.039
Let’s look under the hood and find out together.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.750
This is the input.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:15.420
One colored photograph, that is expected,
and let’s continue…goodness!

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.700
Now this is unexpected… the algorithm creates
a depth map by itself.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:26.520
This depth map tells the algorithm how far
different parts of the image are from the

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:27.520
camera.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.940
Just look at how crisp the outlines are.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.880
My goodness, so good.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:37.690
Then, with this depth information, it now
has an understanding of what is where in this

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.500
image, and creates these layers.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.730
Which is, unfortunately, not much help because,
as you remember, we don’t have any information

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.250
on what is behind the person.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:53.900
No matter, because we can use a technique
that implements image inpainting to fill in

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.300
these regions with sensible data.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.280
And now, with this, we can start exploring
these 3D photos.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.390
So…if it created this depth map from the
color information, this means that we don’t

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.250
even need a depth sensor for this.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.540
Just a simple, color photograph.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:17.310
But wait a minute…this means that we can
plug in any photo from any phone or camera

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:24.400
that we or someone else took, at any time…and
I mean at any time, right?

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.630
Just imagine taking a black and white photo
of a historic event, colorizing it with a

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:35.450
previous learning-based method, and passing
this color image to this new method, and then,

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:36.510
this happens.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.180
My goodness.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:44.340
So, all this looks and sounds great, but how
long do we have to wait for such a 3D photo

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.760
be generated?

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.030
Does my phone battery get completely drained
by the time all this computation is done?

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.370
What is your guess?

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.670
Please stop the video and leave a comment
with your guess.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.270
I’ll wait.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.790
Alright so is this a battery killer?

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.050
Let’s see.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:08.570
The depth estimation step takes…whoa, a
quarter of a second, inpainting, half a second,

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:13.900
and after a little housekeeping, we find out
that this is not a battery killer at all,

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.650
because the whole process is done in approximately
one second.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:19.760
Holy mother of papers.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:35.750
I am very excited to see this technique out
there in 

00:04:35.750 --> 00:05:22.390
the wild as soon as possible.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:26.060
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

